# Expectation record for 13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_long_double_target_restricted
area               = 13_floating_point
description        = long double arithmetic, conversions and comparisons in a folded and a volatile-runtime variant, restricted to same-target comparison because the type's representation was measured to differ across the four backends
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = disabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour.

EVERY VALUE IS EXACTLY REPRESENTABLE IN double, AND THAT IS THE PRECONDITION THE WHOLE PROGRAM RESTS
ON. Every operand and every result is a dyadic rational whose significand fits in 53 bits, so it is
exact in binary64 and therefore exact in the 64-bit x87 significand and the 113-bit binary128
significand alike. That is what allows one golden record to hold for four backends whose long double
formats differ, and it is asserted on the target rather than assumed: the exact_in_double line prints,
per value, whether the result survives a round trip through double unchanged, and every field is 1. A
carelessly chosen value would print 0 and announce itself on the first run instead of silently
invalidating the record. Measured, for contrast: the non-dyadic quotient 1.0L / 3.0L printed at 25
fractional digits gives a different value on the two x87 targets than on the two binary128 targets.

THE FORMAT THE ROUND TRIP RELIES ON IS ESTABLISHED, NOT ASSERTED BY WIDTH. sizeof(double) == 8
establishes that the type occupies eight bytes and nothing more; it establishes neither the radix nor
the significand width, so width alone could not carry this claim. The documented basis for the format
is each target's own ABI -- the System V i386 and x86-64 psABIs,
AAPCS64 and the RISC-V calling convention all specify IEEE binary64 for double -- and the property is
CHECKED at run time by the double_significand line, because a floating comparison is not an integer
constant expression and C11 6.6p6 keeps it out of a static assertion. The probe adds 1/2^52 and 1/2^53
to 1.0 through a volatile double staging object: the first increment must be resolved and the second,
which lies exactly half-way below it, must be absorbed by round-to-nearest-even, and together they pin
the significand at 53 radix-2 bits. The staging object is volatile and of type double for one reason
each and both are essential -- C11 6.3.1.8p2 removes extra range and precision on assignment, and the
volatile qualification makes the store and the reload actually happen -- without which the two x87
targets would evaluate the sums in wider registers and report a different answer from the other two,
a difference in the test rather than in any compiler.

NO NON-FINITE VALUE, NO NEGATIVE ZERO AND NO SUBNORMAL APPEARS. The exponent ranges of the x87 and
binary128 formats differ enormously, and identity properties of those values are not what this program
tests. No division has a zero divisor. The conversions to an integer type truncate 6.25, -6.25 and
12.5, so each integral part sits far inside its destination range on every target, which is what keeps
a floating-to-integer conversion defined under C11 6.3.1.4p1.

NO OBJECT REPRESENTATION IS INSPECTED. A 12-byte or 16-byte object of this type carries padding beyond
its significant bytes, and reading it byte-wise would be both undefined behaviour and target-dependent,
so no long double object is examined byte by byte, copied into an object of another type, or
reinterpreted through overlapping storage or a pointer cast.

No signed integer overflow is possible. No shift appears. No object is read before it is written and no
object is modified twice between sequence points. Every volatile read is staged into a plain local in a
full expression of its own before the call that prints it -- the significand probe included -- so no
call receives a side-effecting argument and nothing depends on the unspecified order in which a call's
arguments are evaluated.

DETERMINISM. Every floating value printed is printed at a fixed precision with margin, and every
value in the program is a dyadic rational -- an integer, or an integer plus a quarter, a half or three
quarters -- so it is exactly representable in binary32 and binary64 alike and no rounding decision is
ever taken. No non-finite value, no negative zero and no subnormal appears anywhere, so no identity
property of those values is relied on. No address, no pointer value, no plain char value and no
target-varying width is printed. Iteration order is fixed by literal bounds. main returns 0, inside
the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is truncated.

HERMETICITY. Every input is a literal in this source. Nothing is opened, nothing is read from the
environment, the command line, the clock or the file system, and no storage is allocated.

GATE. The default warning gate applies unmodified and no `ub_audit_flags` key appears:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror. It is clean under that gate
in the single native compile-only invocation the suite makes of it, clean under the alternate
reference compiler this suite may select in its place, and clean under the one instrumented native
build of -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all. Both gates are
reference-compiler-only.

NO HEADER IS INCLUDED and printf is declared by hand, because bcc ships no standard I/O header.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
ORACLE (b) IS DISABLED FOR THIS PROGRAM, AND THE MEASURED REASON IS THE POINT OF THE PROGRAM.

`long double` has no single representation across the four supported backends. MEASURED:
sizeof(long double) is 16 on x86_64, 12 on i686, 16 on aarch64 and 16 on riscv64; the two x86 targets
carry the x87 80-bit extended format inside that storage while the two others carry IEEE binary128.
Those are different formats with different significand widths, different exponent ranges and different
rounding boundaries. A value difference between two backends over this type is therefore an
IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED difference, which is exactly the carve-out the requirements name -- a
cross-backend divergence not attributable to type widths, char signedness or the ABI is a defect, and
one that IS so attributable is not. Comparing the backends' long double values against one another
would report a permitted difference as a compiler defect, so cross-backend value equality is disabled
here and nowhere else in the corpus.

THE EXCLUSION IS AS NARROW AS THE MECHANISM ALLOWS, AND THE FEATURE IS NOT DROPPED. Constraint C3
forbids excluding a language feature because it is difficult, and nothing is excluded: the program is
written, compiled and executed on ALL FOUR targets at ALL THREE optimization levels, exactly like every
other program in the corpus. Oracle (a) remains enabled, so each backend's output is compared against
the SAME-TARGET reference compiler, which is the comparison that can actually judge it -- a reference
compiler for a given target uses that target's own long double format. Oracle (c) remains enabled, so
every cell is still asserted against the golden record, which catches drift and regression on every
target. What is given up is stated exactly, because understating it would be its own defect: disabling
oracle (b) suppresses ALL NINE of this program's cross-backend comparisons -- each of the three
non-baseline targets against the x86-64 baseline, at each of the three optimization levels. That is
one of the three oracles, for one of the 108 programs, and none of the other 107; it is given up
because those nine comparisons have no meaning over a type with three representations, rather than
because they are inconvenient.

THE GOLDEN RECORD HOLDS ON FOUR TARGETS ANYWAY, WHICH IS WHY ORACLE (c) IS NOT NARROWED TOO. Every
printed value is exactly representable in double and is printed at fixed precision with margin, so all
twelve cells were MEASURED to produce these bytes identically. The exclusion above is therefore about
what the comparison would MEAN, not about a difference this program actually exhibits: the same values
printed by two different formats agree here because they were chosen to, and a program that printed a
non-dyadic long double result would not agree. Recording the restriction on the meaning rather than on
today's measurement is what keeps it correct if a later maintainer adds a value.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT PRINTED. sizeof(long double) never appears as an absolute value -- it is 16,
12, 16 and 16, so printing it would break the shared record on i686 immediately -- and exactly one
target-invariant relation about the type's width is printed instead. No long double characteristic
appears either: no significand digit count, no epsilon, no maximum and no exponent range, because every
one of them differs by target. For the same reason no float.h macro is referenced; that header is
bundled, but every long-double characteristic macro it defines is target-dependent, and exercising the
bundled set is the job of 12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c.

THE EXCLUSION CARRIES THE MARKER XD-TYPE-LONGDOUBLE-001, AND THE TWO ARE ONE STATEMENT RATHER THAN
TWO. The oracle (b) toggle above says WHICH comparison is not made; the marker block below says on
whose authority, under which identifier, and over which cells -- and it is what puts this exclusion
into tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md where the register's bidirectional audit can see it.
A reasoned narrowing with a marker is auditable in one place; the same narrowing justified by prose
alone is auditable nowhere, and it would still be reported XFAIL, so the verdict would claim the
authority of a documented exclusion that no identifier and no resolved basis stood behind. The record
format now refuses that state: a record that disables an oracle without a marker scoping it is
rejected at parse time.

WHAT THE MARKER CLAIMS, PRECISELY. Not that the four backends disagree today -- they do not; all
twelve cells print these bytes, because every printed value was chosen to be exact in all three
representations. What it claims is that a cross-backend VALUE comparison over this type could not be
read as evidence about bcc even if they did disagree, because the three representations are
different formats. That is a statement about what the comparison would MEAN, which is why it stays
correct if a later maintainer adds a value that does diverge -- and it is why the marker can never
reach XPASS: nothing is compared on the arm it scopes, so no divergence can disappear from it.

RETIREMENT. Re-enabling oracle (b) here means deleting the marker block, the register entry and the
impl_defined_notes reason in one edit; narrowing any oracle without all three fails the run, which is
the format declining to let an exclusion become silent.

WIDTH NORMALIZATION ELSEWHERE. The only integer conversions printed are to int and to long long, both
of which have the same width on all four targets, so no target-varying integer width reaches the output
either.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
ld_add=3.750000 ld_sub=1.250000 ld_mul=3.125000 ld_div=1.250000
ld_neg=-2.500000 ld_chain=4.500000 ld_accum=15.875000
ld2d=3.125000 ld2f=2.500000 d2ld=1.500000 i2ld=42.000000
ld2i=6 ld2i_neg=-6 ld2ll=12 ll2ld=1234567.000000
ld_lt=1 ld_gt=1 ld_le_equal=1 ld_ge_equal=1
ld_lt_equal=0 ld_eq=1 ld_ne_equal=0 ld_ne_diff=1
rt_add=3.750000 rt_sub=1.250000 rt_mul=3.125000 rt_div=1.250000
rt_neg=-2.500000 rt_chain=4.500000 rt_accum=15.875000
rt_ld2d=3.125000 rt_ld2f=2.500000 rt_d2ld=1.500000 rt_i2ld=42.000000
rt_ld2i=6 rt_ld2i_neg=-6 rt_ld2ll=12 rt_ll2ld=1234567.000000
rt_lt=1 rt_gt=1 rt_le_equal=1 rt_ge_equal=1
rt_lt_equal=0 rt_eq=1 rt_ne_equal=0 rt_ne_diff=1
folded_matches_runtime=1 1 1 1 1 1 1
folded_matches_runtime_conv=1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1
folded_matches_runtime_cmp=1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1
exact_in_double=1 1 1 1 1 1 1
double_significand=1 1
ld_at_least_double=1
END
expected_divergence.id       = XD-TYPE-LONGDOUBLE-001
expected_divergence.class    = stdout_mismatch
expected_divergence.scope    = oracle_b; all targets; all opt levels
expected_divergence.basis    = docs/technical-specifications.md, line 511, the type representation is specified with target-parametric sizes covering the floating types including long double
expected_divergence.documented <<END
Type representation with target-parametric sizes
END
expected_divergence.observed <<END
The cross-backend value comparison is not attempted for this program: the record disables oracle (b).
What the measurement shows, and what the exclusion rests on, is that sizeof(long double) is 16 on
x86_64, 12 on i686, 16 on aarch64 and 16 on riscv64, with the two x86 targets carrying the x87 80-bit
extended format inside that storage and the other two carrying IEEE binary128 -- three different
formats, with different significand widths, exponent ranges and rounding boundaries. A value
difference between two backends over this type is therefore an implementation-defined difference of
the kind the brief's carve-out names, not a defect in either.
END
